I wanted to add "Was this useful?" buttons to my blog posts -- thumbs up, thumbs down, maybe a star rating. The site runs on GitHub Pages. No backend. No database. No server-side anything. And I didn't want JavaScript in the interaction itself.

The solution turned out to be 30 years old.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - A Cloudflare Worker handles feedback clicks via HTTP redirect -- no client-side JS needed
> - The pattern is identical to how 1994 web rings counted click-throughs
> - CSS `:target` shows a "Thanks!" message after the redirect without page navigation
> - An SVG badge displays live feedback counts -- the 1996 hit counter, reborn
> - [PLACEHOLDER: Link to the Worker repo or npm package once published]

## The constraint that made it interesting

This site already uses [a CSS-only toggle](/posts/20250906-css-naked-css-only/) to disable all styles with a checkbox. The search form works without JavaScript. [URLs carry state](/posts/20251226-url-state-management/) instead of client-side stores. The pattern is clear: HTML and CSS do the work; JavaScript is an enhancement, not a dependency.

Feedback buttons should follow the same rule. Click a link, register the vote, done. No `fetch()` calls, no framework, no hydration. If JavaScript fails to load, the feedback still works.

The problem is obvious: GitHub Pages serves static files. There's nowhere for the vote to go.

## What a 1998 web developer would do

In 1998, your personal page on GeoCities was static HTML -- just like GitHub Pages today. You had no backend. But you wanted interactivity: hit counters, guestbooks, polls. So you used other people's CGI scripts. An `<img>` tag could point to a script on someone else's server that generated a counter image. A `<form>` action could submit to a remote CGI script that appended to a flat file and redirected back.

The web was always a composition of origins. Your static page was the frontend; the internet was the backend.

Web rings used this exact architecture. The "Next site" button was a link to a central CGI script:

```
http://www.webring.org/cgi-bin/webring?ring=navships;id=002;next
```

Click it, and the script would increment a counter, look up the next site, and return a `302 Found` redirect. The user barely noticed the hop. This pattern worked in Mosaic. It works in every browser ever made. It's pure HTTP.

Replace "next site in the ring" with "the post you just read" and "click-through count" with "thumbs-up count." The architecture is identical.

## The redirect-count-redirect pattern

Here's what happens when a reader clicks the thumbs-up button:

```
Reader clicks "👍 Yes" on a blog post
  → GET https://feedback.allaboutken.com/up/posts/my-post/
  → Worker increments counter in KV storage
  → Worker returns: 302 Location: https://www.allaboutken.com/posts/my-post/#thanks
  → Browser follows redirect back to the same post
  → CSS :target reveals "Thanks for the feedback!" message
  → Reader never truly left the page
```

The `#thanks` fragment in the redirect URL is doing real work. It's [URL-as-state](/posts/20251226-url-state-management/) again -- the fragment triggers a `:target` CSS rule that reveals a thank-you message that was always in the HTML, just hidden. No JavaScript needed to show it.

The Worker itself is around 50 lines. It handles three routes:

- `/up/posts/slug/` and `/down/posts/slug/` -- increment a KV counter, redirect back
- `/count/posts/slug.svg` -- return a dynamic SVG badge showing the current count

That SVG endpoint is the 1996 hit counter pattern. A `<img>` tag pointing to a script that returns a dynamically generated image:

```html
<img src="https://feedback.allaboutken.com/count/posts/my-post.svg"
     alt="47 people found this useful">
```

The browser thinks it's loading a normal image. The Worker is secretly running code. This is exactly what `<img src="/cgi-bin/counter.pl">` did in 1996, except the "CGI script" runs on a global edge network instead of a Pentium II under someone's desk.

## Standing on shoulders

I'm not the first person to think of this. Two projects proved different halves of the solution:

**[vberlier/poll](https://github.com/vberlier/poll)** is a Cloudflare Worker (written in Rust, MIT-licensed) that implements the redirect-count-redirect pattern with SVG result widgets. It's the exact 1994 web ring architecture applied to voting. No client-side JavaScript. It proved the interaction pattern works.

**Andrew Walpole** [built a like button](https://andrewwalpole.com/blog/building-a-like-button-with-cloudflare-workers/) for his Eleventy blog using Cloudflare Workers and KV storage. He deployed it, ran it in production, and wrote about the experience. It proved the Eleventy + Workers + KV infrastructure works. But his approach uses client-side JavaScript -- petite-vue renders the button, and `fetch()` API calls communicate with the Worker.

Nobody had combined them. Walpole's infrastructure with vberlier's interaction pattern -- a feedback system for a static Eleventy site that works without a single line of client-side JavaScript.

## Keeping bots honest

The obvious concern: what stops a crawler from wandering onto `/feedback/up/posts/my-post/` and registering a false vote?

Five layers of defense, from prevention to cleanup:

1. **Don't advertise the paths.** `robots.txt` disallows `/feedback/`. The sitemap excludes feedback URLs. Feedback links carry `rel="nofollow"`.
2. **Tell arrivals to leave.** Feedback pages include `<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">`.
3. **Server-side filtering.** The Worker checks `User-Agent` against known bot signatures and validates request headers. Simple crawlers don't send `Referer` or `Accept-Language` headers that real browsers do.
4. **Rate limiting.** One vote per IP per post within a time window, enforced by the Worker. [PLACEHOLDER: Confirm rate limiting approach -- Workers Rate Limiting binding vs. KV-based TTL]
5. **Data cleanup.** KV counters can be reset with `wrangler kv:key delete`. If a bot swarm hits, zero the affected counters and move on.

The site previously used a GoatCounter `<noscript>` tracking pixel. That's actually a bot magnet -- simple crawlers load images but skip JavaScript. [PLACEHOLDER: Confirm whether noscript pixel was removed]

## Privacy by design

No consent banner needed. The Worker increments a counter in KV storage -- no IP addresses stored, no User-Agent logged, no cookies set. [GoatCounter's GDPR analysis](https://www.goatcounter.com/help/gdpr) applies here with even more confidence: if aggregate-only page view counting falls under legitimate interest, then an anonymous integer counter certainly does.

